#{[17592186278298 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={v}^{2}+uv$ and $y(u,v)=v-u.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v+u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & 2v+u \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-(2v+u)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =v+2v+u \\\\  & =3v+u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(8,-2)}=3(-2)+8=2.\n$$"] [17592186278300 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡v-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{6},0)}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186278358 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{e}^{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278359 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278360 "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }3r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}(\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{2}-0)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (1-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[(\\operatorname{sin}\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{sin}0-\\frac{1}{3}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[(1-\\frac{1}{3})-0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278361 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/9b/9b54b7a488a1983ba1816fdf5c55e039951eb417102c61963977b2f48b9c8d9e.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}12{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}15({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}15{r}^{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )(1-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-({\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}(2\\pi )-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}(0)) \\\\  & =-(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278363 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/0a/0a1de805051eff4ea82b4eefb7bb113c724b3d4e7304704831b6294ee4e4d674.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}4(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{r}^{3}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}4{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}{[{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta (81-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}4\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278364 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/81/811cfa127ad232e8d5a990998604718a57344d1bd7f43173dc0987631c13db33.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{e}^{(r{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{e}^{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.222em}{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{r}^{2}}]}_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\pi ({e}^{4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278365 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/0a/0a1de805051eff4ea82b4eefb7bb113c724b3d4e7304704831b6294ee4e4d674.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}2({(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}2{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.222em}{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{81}{4}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278366 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }(2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -0)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278369 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/f0/f0417011f46d3860b7de86fe652a7d80dd9787cb05c2057601b41c17e0c2b423.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -1)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/3f/3f4bb1428a6cc9b7e191e1904636d654145d9a1f2fb1dd40843d8cd878194772.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/d6/d6f4e054767650cd2a3d348aee8210af421e2df21792d79f42c5a58b5f4e82e6.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278370 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/86/86036fd86096355b969da695f812c9bf835880edd68f7991183b70a6234e3eb4.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/9e/9ef22dcc15175664331d989bd50d9a8d60a354a0780dbaf9465caca04f28c2d9.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/f7/f724daf8321195815c9e413aab2106c611bc9d2e9653939266fc828aebeca68c.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{r}^{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278371 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/d8/d882bf70ecb7b94eee0eeeffbf63874ca8607268167f96278deb4678ec3b827a.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(4,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -4)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -8r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +16+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{